      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, sorry about that! I got a little carried away with the goblin metaphor, but don't worry, the goblins are just trying to help you keep your numbers straight, even if they are a bit chaotic.\n\nTo answer your question about the **Draw vs. Equipment** categorization: If you put that expensive chair in your **Owner's Draw** (or Drawings) account, you are essentially treating it as money you took out of your business for personal use. This means you **cannot deduct the cost of the chair as a business expense** on your taxes. You would have to pay income tax on the full purchase price as if you withdrew that much cash for personal enjoyment, which is a huge tax hit!\n\nInstead, you should categorize it under **Equipment** or **Furniture**. This way, the chair is considered a business asset. You can then depreciate it over time (usually 7 years for furniture under MACRS rules) or use Section 179 to deduct a portion of the cost immediately, depending on your specific situation and QuickBooks version.\n\nJust remember, if you accidentally put it in Draw, you might need to do a journal entry to move the cost to Equipment and adjust",
